+[F1] A compact smooth metric-extended surface region has a finite boundary-relative curvilinear triangulation, including a closed region. Smooth boundary collars exist under Countable Choice. Cellular approximation of a based circle map to a supplied finite CW complex is homotopic relative to its vertex into the one-skeleton. Smooth nonsingular real maps have smooth local inverses. [[thm-finite-curvilinear-triangulation-of-a-compact-riemannian-surface]], [[thm-collar-neighborhood-theorem]], [[thm-cellular-approximation-for-maps-of-cw-pairs]], [[lem-choice-free-smooth-inverse-function-theorem-in-euclidean-space]]. This curvilinear provider's actual proof5.1 and finite planar fan retain additional finite boundary marks by common subdivision. Its regular $C^2$ edges, triangular closed-disk faces and circle/interval links give the finite regular CW structure used here.
 
 [F2] Zeros of a nonzero holomorphic function are isolated, by the identity theorem; applying it to local quadratic coefficients gives finitely many zeros on any compact neighborhood of $K$. Near a nonzero coefficient its square root exists by the holomorphic inverse theorem for $z\mapsto z^2$, integration on a small coordinate disc gives a primitive, and the nonzero derivative makes that primitive a local coordinate. Thus at every nonzero point there is an embedded natural coordinate in which $q=dw^2$. [[thm-identity-theorem-holomorphic-functions]], [[thm-holomorphic-inverse-function-theorem]], [[thm-holomorphic-primitive-on-star-shaped-domain]]
 
